import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.D66Xq0Kv.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Themes","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/themes.md","filePath":"guide/themes.md"}'),s={name:"guide/themes.md"};function n(h,e,i,c,l,d){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="themes" tabindex="-1">Themes <a class="header-anchor" href="#themes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Themes&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A theme sets every color of the console: pages, cards, buttons, charts, the mission card, the illustrations and Ted. Orbit generates a whole theme from three colors and checks that text, controls and charts stay readable in light and dark mode, so a theme you make is as easy to read as the ones Orbit ships.</p><p>Everything below lives in <strong>Settings &gt; General &gt; Appearance</strong>.</p><h2 id="pick" tabindex="-1">Pick a theme <a class="header-anchor" href="#pick" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pick a theme {#pick}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Orbit</strong>, <strong>Tide</strong> and <strong>High contrast</strong> are Orbit&#39;s own themes. Eight more follow them, from <strong>Midnight</strong> to <strong>Mint</strong>, each made from three colors. Choosing one applies it at once to the whole console and saves it to your account.</p><p><strong>Light</strong>, <strong>Dark</strong> and <strong>System</strong> at the bottom of the card choose the mode. Every theme has both modes, and changing the mode never changes your theme.</p><h2 id="make" tabindex="-1">Make your own <a class="header-anchor" href="#make" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Make your own {#make}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Under <strong>Your colors</strong>, pick:</p><ul><li><strong>Background</strong>: the page color. A dark color is kept exactly in dark mode and Orbit makes a light page that matches it; a light color works the other way around.</li><li><strong>Buttons and links</strong>: the main color, also used for Ted&#39;s suit, the planet and the charts.</li><li><strong>Secondary accent</strong>: badges, the mission card and the other highlights.</li></ul><p>The console changes while you drag, and the choice is saved when you let go. The two previews show the theme in light and in dark mode, and the line under them tells you whether every text and control reads clearly in both. <strong>Undo</strong> steps back through your last changes; <strong>Back to the theme&#39;s colors</strong> clears your colors. Give the theme a name to recognize it in exports and in the workspace.</p><p>When you only choose buttons or the accent and keep the background, Orbit keeps the rest of the theme you started from.</p><h2 id="fine-tune" tabindex="-1">Fine-tune a color <a class="header-anchor" href="#fine-tune" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Fine-tune a color {#fine-tune}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Fine-tune each color</strong> lists the colors of the console by group: grounds, text, brand, status, the mission card, the sidebar, illustrations and charts. Choose <strong>Light</strong> or <strong>Dark</strong> to edit that mode; a color set here belongs to one mode.</p><p>Next to each color that sits on another one, a badge shows the contrast between them and turns red below what it needs: 4.5:1 for text and controls, 7:1 for the mission card&#39;s text, 3:1 for borders and charts. <strong>Fix</strong> picks the nearest color that reaches it. <strong>Automatic</strong> goes back to the generated color. While you tune, the settings card keeps the generated colors, so you can always read your way back.</p><h2 id="share" tabindex="-1">Share a theme <a class="header-anchor" href="#share" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Share a theme {#share}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Copy theme code</strong> copies a line that starts with <code>orbit-theme:</code>. Anyone on any Orbit installation can paste it into <strong>Theme code</strong> and choose <strong>Use this theme</strong> to wear the same theme, in their own mode. The code holds only colors and the name; nothing else about you or your installation travels with it. If copying is blocked by the browser, Orbit shows the code to copy by hand.</p><h2 id="workspace" tabindex="-1">A theme for the whole workspace <a class="header-anchor" href="#workspace" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A theme for the whole workspace {#workspace}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Administrators can give a workspace the theme they are wearing: choose <strong>Use my theme for</strong> followed by the workspace&#39;s name. From then on:</p><ul><li>people who never chose a theme see the workspace&#39;s, in their own light or dark mode;</li><li>people with a theme of their own keep it, and can choose <strong>Use the workspace theme</strong> at any time;</li><li>switching workspaces in the sidebar switches the theme with it, without reloading.</li></ul><p>Changing the mode while you follow the workspace keeps you on its theme. Changing any color makes a theme of your own, and <strong>Use the workspace theme</strong> brings the workspace&#39;s back. <strong>Remove the workspace theme</strong> takes it away; people who followed it go back to Orbit&#39;s default or to their own theme.</p><p>The theme is part of the workspace, so the API returns it in <code>GET /workspaces</code> and administrators set it with <code>PUT /workspaces/{workspaceId}/appearance</code>.</p>',21)])])}const p=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{m as __pageData,p as default};
